Overlay patch 0051: re-key MK64's camera-facing work to the COMPOSED EYE,
and stand down the remaining angle culls in VR (VR-spec D5/D6, traps
A2/A3/A7/D6).

MK64 aims three separate families of things at "the camera", and in VR the
head owns none of them. The shell exports the composed eye — derived from the
very A*V*P matrix the eyes render with, which is trap A7's rule (RaYRoD burned
three sm64 patches on a separately-derived eye-facing that over-rotated until
sprites went edge-on) — and every site below reads it instead:

  1. POSITIONAL billboards. player_controller.c computes each kart's billboard
     yaw as atan2s(player->pos - camera->pos): the sprite faces the camera
     POSITION. In first person the eye is metres away from the chase camera,
     so the karts beside and behind you face the wrong way. Re-keyed to
     gSohVREyePosGame (the eye, in GAME units).

  2. YAW-KEYED billboards. render_course_actors builds sBillBoardMtx from
     camera->rot[1] — one Y-rotation shared by every item box, tree and crowd
     sprite in the course. Re-keyed to gSohVREyeYawBam.

  3. THE SKY (trap D6). SkyCloud/SkyStar place their screen-space sprites at
     x = 160 + (1.7578125 / (fov + 40)) * (camera->rot[1] + rotY) — a linear
     yaw -> screen-x mapping. Turn your head in VR and unhandled clouds smear
     and swim. Re-keyed to the eye yaw AND rebuilt on the EYE frustum's own
     horizontal FOV (gSohVREyeFovDeg), which is what the sky is actually being
     rasterised into. Only in gSohVRSkyMode 1 (first person): the Diorama mode
     still parks the sky in the HUD framebuffer and wipes it, so its sprites
     are never drawn at all.

  4. ANGLE CULLS. 0048 already widened the one in is_within_render_distance
     (the chokepoint ~25 cull sites funnel through). code_80086E70.c carries
     three MORE angle tests that do not — is_object_visible_on_camera and its
     two siblings — and they are the reason rear geometry still went missing
     at 180 degrees. Same rule as 0048, verbatim: view-direction culling
     stands down in VR, distance culling stays.

R16-B (rev6) replaces the sky's LINEAR placement with the TANGENT one, in the
same block rev2 owns (a regeneration, never a new patch: traps D18/D22).
docs/vr-r16-diagnosis/clouds.md is the derivation; the short version is that
rev2 fixed the RATE at the frame CENTRE and left the MAPPING linear, so a cloud
carried to the rim of this device's 105-degree eye by a head turn slides ~10
degrees against the trees under it and a quarter of the frame width. Three
changes, all inside the `gSohVREyeKey && gSohVRSkyMode` branch:

  * PLACEMENT. mX = 160*(1 + (2*tan(cameraRot) - tanSum)/span), with tanSum and
    span the shell's published tangents for EYE 0 -- the one reference frustum
    the single sprite layout is built in. Its value AND slope at cameraRot = 0
    are rev2's published centre and rate exactly, so this is a strict
    generalisation of what shipped rather than a replacement for it. Guarded at
    85 degrees, where tanf explodes and a sprite has no finite projection.
  * BOUNDS. The fov-derived mUnk208/mUnk210 keep sprites 17 degrees past the
    edge of a frustum that only reaches +45 (they read +/-77 against an eye
    that sees -60..+45), which with the linear compression is why clouds crowd
    the rim and linger there. The VR branch culls on the eye's OWN tangents
    instead -- the UNION of both eyes plus a sprite half-width, because a sprite
    culled out of one eye only is a stereo-rivalry bug of its own.
  * THE POSE IT IS BUILT WITH. rev2 read gSohVREyeYawBam, a global the
    compositor rewrites at 90-120 Hz, from the GAME update at ~28 Hz -- so the
    clouds were keyed to a head pose the world's own matrices never used
    (clouds.md M3: 3-9 degrees of slide WHILE THE HEAD IS MOVING, which is
    the user's report to the letter). rev6 reads the R16-A frame SNAPSHOT's
    mirror instead, and records the yaw it built with in gSohVRSkyBuiltYawBam
    so 0044 rev11 can difference it against the frame it actually draws and
    fold the remainder into the same per-eye term. `vr set skylatch 0` restores
    the live read on BOTH sides at once, which is the red control.

gSohVRSkyTan is the A/B: 0 leaves rev2's lines standing untouched, which is the
1.0.0.12 behaviour the off-centre falsifier must FAIL on. gSohVRSkyRate 0 still
restores the 1.0.0.11 linear mapping ahead of both.

R17-B (rev7) STOPS CORRECTING THE ORTHO LAYOUT AND REPLACES IT (clouds.md §7's
H5). the user, on 1.0.0.13 -- the fourth build to correct this sky: "the clouds in
my vr world still move around as i move my head, they even rotate as i rotate my
head, meaning i can make a cloud turn on its side. plus they shake in place as i
look left and right." Three residuals, all structural to CORRECTING a
screen-space layout rather than replacing it: the rim residual of a first-order
dtheta term; roll, which the ortho path cannot express per sprite; and the
30 Hz staircase of a layout rebuilt at GAME rate under a correction applied at
COMPOSITOR rate.

In gSohVRSkyDome each cloud and star is emitted as a WORLD-SPACE BILLBOARD on a
dome of radius gSohVRSkyDomeR centred on the eye, through the ordinary matrix
stack, and DrawSkyActors (overlay 0058 rev5) re-loads the game's own world->clip
projection for the span -- so the eye pass substitutes the SAME per-eye A*V*P it
draws the trees with. M1-M5 vanish by construction: no yaw->pixel mapping to get
wrong, each eye projects its own frustum, roll falls out, and the sprites' world
positions do not depend on the head at all, so nothing can shake when the head
moves. The conversion from MK64's authored (rotY, posY, scalePercent) to
(azimuth, elevation, angular size) is derived in the code comment beside
soh_ios_sky_dir; the SIGN of the azimuth is the game's own mapping and nothing
else. `vr set skydome 0` restores every rev6 line above within the same game
frame, on the same binary, and that is this round's red control (trap D20).

R4 (rev3) adds the PHYSICAL THROW's vote (spec D13). MK64 already makes a
binary forward/backward decision at item release, reading the stick; the Sense
gesture layer casts that same vote from the hand's velocity at the instant of
release. The three decision sites are the SAME three rev2 instruments — which
is why this is a regeneration and not a new patch — and each keeps its original
stick test intact: the wrapper only overrides when the shell has actually voted
(gSohVRThrowDir != 0), so a gesture that declines to vote, a gamepad player, and
every non-VR build all behave exactly as before.

R3 (rev2) adds one more export, for the rear-view pane's auto-show
(spec D12): gSohVRRearItemSeq, a monotone counter bumped whenever the
1P kart puts an item out BEHIND it. MK64 makes the distinction cleanly and
in exactly three places, all of them a released HELD item:

  * a held green shell released with the stick pulled back past
    THROW_SHELL_BACKWARDS (-45) is thrown BACKWARD — counted. Released
    without that, the same shell becomes RELEASED_SHELL and fires FORWARD
    — not counted, which is the spec's "a forward fire does not".
  * a dragged banana released without the stick forward is dropped behind
    — counted. With the stick forward (>30, small X) it is thrown ahead —
    not counted.
  * the same test on a banana-bunch banana: drop = counted, forward throw
    = not.

Red and blue shells have no backward release in MK64 (they always leave
forward), so there is nothing to instrument there. Player 1 only: the
pane is the 1P kart's mirror.

The eye-facing gate is gSohVREyeKey, which the loop sets ONLY for VR world
frames and ONLY while the game is not paused — trap A7's second half (the
donor's pause screen inherited eye-facing and broke). Flat, 3D-panel and iOS
builds never set it, so they are byte-identical in behaviour.

All exports are shell-owned globals (SohIosShell.m); extern "C" decls at FILE
scope in the C++ TUs (block-scope externs namespace-mangle — the project's
recurring linkage trap). __IOS__-guarded: the macOS build links no shell.


